SOMETHING OUTSIDE REACT


================================================================================
PART 2: UNDERSTANDING SIDE EFFECTS
================================================================================

WHAT IS A SIDE EFFECT?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A side effect is work that interacts with something outside the pure rendering
calculation of a React component.

Examples:

• HTTP requests

• Browser storage

• Timers

• Browser event listeners

• WebSocket connections

• Third-party libraries

• Analytics

• Notifications

• Geolocation

• Direct integration with browser APIs


================================================================================
EXAMPLES
================================================================================

HTTP REQUEST:

fetch(
  '/api/groceries'
)


LOCAL STORAGE:

localStorage.setItem(
  'groceries',
  JSON.stringify(items)
)


TIMER:

setTimeout(
  callback,
  1000
)


EVENT LISTENER:

window.addEventListener(
  'resize',
  handleResize
)


WEBSOCKET:

const socket =
  new WebSocket(
    url
  )


DOCUMENT API:

document.title =
  'Grocery List'


================================================================================
SIDE EFFECT TABLE
================================================================================

+----------------------+--------------------------------+-----------------------+
| SIDE EFFECT          | EXAMPLE                        | PURPOSE               |
+----------------------+--------------------------------+-----------------------+
| HTTP Request         | fetch()                        | Backend communication |
| Storage              | localStorage.setItem()         | Browser persistence   |
| Event Listener       | addEventListener()             | Observe browser event |
| Timer                | setTimeout()                   | Delayed work          |
| Interval             | setInterval()                  | Repeated work         |
| Subscription         | subscribe()                    | External updates      |
| WebSocket            | new WebSocket()                | Real-time connection  |
| Analytics            | analytics.track()              | Usage tracking        |
| Document API         | document.title                 | Browser integration   |
| Geolocation          | navigator.geolocation          | Device/location API   |
+----------------------+--------------------------------+-----------------------+


================================================================================
SIDE EFFECTS SHOULD NOT RUN DURING RENDER
================================================================================

Avoid:

function GroceryList() {

  fetch(
    '/api/groceries'
  )

  return (
    ...
  )
}


Why?

The component function can run whenever React renders.

That could cause:

Render
  ↓
fetch()
  ↓
State Update
  ↓
Render
  ↓
fetch()
  ↓
State Update
  ↓
...


Instead, synchronize with the external API using an appropriate Effect or
data-fetching architecture.


================================================================================
IMPORTANT: NOT EVERY ACTION NEEDS useEffect
================================================================================

Suppose the user clicks:

Delete Item


You already know WHY the deletion should happen:

THE USER CLICKED DELETE


Therefore the operation can normally begin from the event handler.


Example:

const handleDelete = () => {

  deleteItem(
    item.id
  )
}


You do NOT need to create state merely so an Effect notices it and performs
the delete.


RULE:

USER EVENT
    ↓
EVENT HANDLER


EXTERNAL SYNCHRONIZATION CAUSED BY RENDERED STATE
    ↓
EFFECT


================================================================================
SIDE EFFECT EXAMPLES IN OUR GROCERY APP
================================================================================

Possible future side effects:

Load Groceries
      ↓
GET /api/groceries


Add Grocery
      ↓
POST /api/groceries


Update Purchased State
      ↓
PATCH /api/groceries/{id}


Delete Grocery
      ↓
DELETE /api/groceries/{id}


Persist Locally
      ↓
localStorage


These operations connect our React application to systems outside React.


================================================================================
PART 3: UNDERSTANDING REACT HOOKS
================================================================================

WHAT ARE REACT HOOKS?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Hooks are functions that let function components use React features.

Examples:

• State

• Context

• Effects

• References

• Reducers

• Memoization


================================================================================
COMMON REACT HOOKS
================================================================================

useState
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Stores component state.


Example:

const [
  items,
  setItems
] = useState([])


useEffect
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Synchronizes a component with an external system.


Example:

useEffect(() => {

  // Synchronization

}, [])


useContext
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Reads a React Context value.


useReducer
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Manages state using reducer logic.


useRef
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Stores a mutable reference that does not itself cause rendering when changed.

It is also commonly used to reference DOM elements.


useMemo
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Caches a calculated value when appropriate.


useCallback
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Caches a function reference when appropriate.


useId
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Generates IDs useful for accessibility relationships.


useLayoutEffect
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Runs an effect in a timing suitable for layout measurement or synchronous
visual adjustments before the browser paints.


================================================================================
HOOK SUMMARY
================================================================================

+------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| HOOK             | PRIMARY PURPOSE                               |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| useState         | Component state                               |
| useEffect        | External synchronization                      |
| useContext       | Read context                                  |
| useReducer       | Reducer-based state                           |
| useRef           | Persistent mutable/DOM reference              |
| useMemo          | Cache calculated value                        |
| useCallback      | Cache function reference                      |
| useId            | Stable accessibility IDs                      |
| useLayoutEffect  | Layout-related synchronization                |
+------------------+-----------------------------------------------+


================================================================================
RULES OF HOOKS
================================================================================

Hooks should be called:

• At the top level of React components

• At the top level of custom Hooks


Avoid calling Hooks conditionally:

if (condition) {

  useEffect(...)
}


Avoid:

for (...) {

  useState(...)
}


Avoid:

function handleClick() {

  useEffect(...)
}


Instead:

function MyComponent() {

  const [state, setState] =
    useState(...)


  useEffect(() => {

    ...

  }, [])


  ...
}


================================================================================
CUSTOM HOOKS
================================================================================

Reusable Hook logic can be extracted into custom Hooks.

Examples:

useGroceries()

useWindowSize()

useLocalStorage()

useUser()


Custom Hooks normally begin with:

use


================================================================================
PART 4: UNDERSTANDING useEffect
================================================================================

WHAT IS useEffect?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

useEffect is a React Hook used to synchronize a component with an external
system.

Basic structure:

useEffect(() => {

  // Setup / synchronization logic


  return () => {

    // Optional cleanup

  }

}, [
  dependencies
])


================================================================================
IMPORT
================================================================================

import {
  useEffect
} from 'react'


With state:

import {
  useEffect,
  useState
} from 'react'


================================================================================
BASIC EXAMPLE
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  document.title =
    'Grocery List'

}, [])


Effect:

Component renders
      ↓
React commits update
      ↓
Effect executes
      ↓
Browser title synchronized


================================================================================
DEPENDENCY ARRAY
================================================================================

Example:

useEffect(() => {

  fetchUser(
    userId
  )

}, [
  userId
])


Meaning:

This effect depends on:

userId


When the relevant dependency changes:

React performs the effect lifecycle again.


================================================================================
CLEANUP
================================================================================

Example:

useEffect(() => {

  window.addEventListener(
    'resize',
    handleResize
  )


  return () => {

    window.removeEventListener(
      'resize',
      handleResize
    )

  }

}, [])


Pattern:

SETUP
  ↓
USE RESOURCE
  ↓
CLEANUP


================================================================================
PART 5: useEffect DEPENDENCY ARRAYS
================================================================================

CASE 1: NO DEPENDENCY ARRAY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

useEffect(() => {

  console.log(
    'Effect'
  )

})


This effect runs after every committed render where the Effect is scheduled.


Do NOT say:

"Never use this."


Sometimes it is intentional.

But usually you should understand why an Effect needs to run after every
render before using this form.


================================================================================
CASE 2: EMPTY DEPENDENCY ARRAY
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  console.log(
    'Initial synchronization'
  )

}, [])


Normal conceptual model:

Run after initial mounting.


Useful when synchronization does not depend on changing reactive values.


================================================================================
IMPORTANT DEVELOPMENT NOTE
================================================================================

Do NOT memorize:

[] = GUARANTEED EXACTLY ONCE


In development, React Strict Mode can intentionally perform additional setup
and cleanup cycles to help expose effect bugs.


Therefore think:

[] =
NO REACTIVE DEPENDENCIES


rather than:

[] =
EXACTLY ONE EXECUTION UNDER EVERY ENVIRONMENT


================================================================================
CASE 3: ONE DEPENDENCY
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  fetchUserData(
    userId
  )

}, [
  userId
])


Effect setup runs:

• After mounting

• Again when userId changes


Before rerunning an existing Effect, React runs its previous cleanup when one
was returned.


================================================================================
CASE 4: MULTIPLE DEPENDENCIES
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  searchProducts(
    userId,
    category
  )

}, [
  userId,
  category
])


The Effect responds when one of its reactive dependencies changes.


================================================================================
DEPENDENCY TABLE
================================================================================

+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| DEPENDENCIES         | CONCEPTUAL BEHAVIOR                           |
+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| omitted              | Effect after every relevant render            |
| []                   | No changing reactive dependencies              |
| [userId]             | Mount + userId changes                        |
| [userId, category]   | Mount + either dependency changes             |
+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+


================================================================================
DEPENDENCIES ARE NOT JUST A SCHEDULING TRICK
================================================================================

Don't think:

"I will remove userId from the dependency array because I only want the
Effect to run once."


Instead ask:

DOES THE EFFECT READ userId?


If yes, then:

userId

is part of the Effect's reactive logic.


Example:

useEffect(() => {

  fetchUserData(
    userId
  )

}, [
  userId
])


================================================================================
INFINITE LOOP EXAMPLE
================================================================================

Be careful with effects that update state used by their own dependencies.


Example:

useEffect(() => {

  setCount(
    count + 1
  )

}, [
  count
])


Flow:

count changes
   ↓
Effect
   ↓
setCount()
   ↓
count changes
   ↓
Effect
   ↓
...


This creates a loop.


================================================================================
PART 6: useEffect FOR API CALLS
================================================================================

We can use an Effect when a component needs to synchronize displayed data with
a backend resource identified by a prop.


Example:

UserProfile
+
userId


When:

userId changes


we need:

new backend data.


================================================================================
UserProfile.jsx
================================================================================

import {
  useEffect,
  useState
} from 'react'


export default function UserProfile({

  userId

}) {

  // ============================================
  // STATE
  // ============================================

  const [
    user,
    setUser
  ] = useState(null)


  const [
    loading,
    setLoading
  ] = useState(false)


  const [
    error,
    setError
  ] = useState(null)


  // ============================================
  // EFFECT
  // ============================================

  useEffect(() => {

    if (!userId) {

      setUser(null)

      setLoading(false)

      setError(null)

      return
    }


    const controller =
      new AbortController()


    const fetchUserData =
      async () => {

        try {

          setLoading(true)

          setError(null)


          const response =
            await fetch(
              `/api/users/${userId}`,
              {
                signal:
                  controller.signal
              }
            )


          if (!response.ok) {

            throw new Error(
              `Request failed with status ${response.status}`
            )
          }


          const data =
            await response.json()


          setUser(
            data
          )


        } catch (error) {

          if (
            error.name ===
            'AbortError'
          ) {

            return
          }


          setUser(null)


          setError(
            error instanceof Error
              ? error.message
              : 'Unable to load user'
          )


        } finally {

          if (
            !controller.signal.aborted
          ) {

            setLoading(false)
          }

        }
      }


    fetchUserData()


    return () => {

      controller.abort()

    }

  }, [
    userId
  ])


  // ============================================
  // RENDER
  // ============================================

  if (loading) {

    return (
      <p role="status">

        Loading user data...

      </p>
    )
  }


  if (error) {

    return (
      <p role="alert">

        Error:
        {' '}
        {error}

      </p>
    )
  }


  if (!user) {

    return (
      <p>
        No user data.
      </p>
    )
  }


  return (
    <section className="user-profile">

      <h1>
        {user.name}
      </h1>

      <p>
        Email:
        {' '}
        {user.email}
      </p>

      <p>
        Phone:
        {' '}
        {user.phone}
      </p>

    </section>
  )
}


================================================================================
API FLOW
================================================================================

Component Render
      ↓
Effect Runs
      ↓
Loading = true
      ↓
fetch()
      ↓
Backend API
      ↓
HTTP Response
      ↓
response.ok?
   /       \
 NO        YES
 ↓          ↓
Error     response.json()
            ↓
          Data
            ↓
        setUser(data)
            ↓
          Render


================================================================================
WHY NOT MAKE THE EFFECT CALLBACK async?
================================================================================

Avoid:

useEffect(
  async () => {

    const response =
      await fetch(...)

  },
  []
)


The Effect callback can return:

• Nothing

or

• A cleanup function


An `async` function returns a Promise.


Instead:

useEffect(() => {

  const loadData =
    async () => {

      ...
    }


  loadData()


  return () => {

    ...

  }

}, [])


================================================================================
FETCH DOES NOT AUTOMATICALLY REJECT ALL HTTP ERRORS
================================================================================

A network failure may reject:

fetch()


But an HTTP response such as:

404

or:

500


still produces a Response object.


Therefore check:

if (!response.ok) {

  throw new Error(...)

}


================================================================================
LOADING STATE
================================================================================

Before request:

loading =
true


After a successful/error response:

loading =
false


UI can display:

Loading user data...


================================================================================
ERROR STATE
================================================================================

If request fails:

error =
message


UI:

Error:
Request failed...


================================================================================
SUCCESS STATE
================================================================================

If request succeeds:

user =
data


UI renders:

user.name

user.email

user.phone


