PART 7: useEffect CLEANUP FUNCTION
================================================================================

WHAT IS CLEANUP?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

An Effect may create a resource that needs to be stopped, removed, disconnected,
or cancelled.


Examples:

• Event listener

• Timer

• Subscription

• WebSocket

• Network request

• MutationObserver


Cleanup reverses that setup.


================================================================================
GENERAL PATTERN
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  // Setup


  return () => {

    // Cleanup

  }

}, [
  dependencies
])


================================================================================
EXAMPLE 1: EVENT LISTENER
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const handleResize = () => {

    console.log(
      window.innerWidth
    )
  }


  window.addEventListener(
    'resize',
    handleResize
  )


  return () => {

    window.removeEventListener(
      'resize',
      handleResize
    )

  }

}, [])


SETUP:

addEventListener()


CLEANUP:

removeEventListener()


================================================================================
EXAMPLE 2: TIMER
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const timerId =
    setTimeout(
      () => {

        console.log(
          'Timer finished'
        )

      },
      1000
    )


  return () => {

    clearTimeout(
      timerId
    )

  }

}, [])


SETUP:

setTimeout()


CLEANUP:

clearTimeout()


================================================================================
EXAMPLE 3: INTERVAL
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const intervalId =
    setInterval(
      () => {

        console.log(
          'Refresh'
        )

      },
      5000
    )


  return () => {

    clearInterval(
      intervalId
    )

  }

}, [])


================================================================================
EXAMPLE 4: SUBSCRIPTION
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const unsubscribe =
    dataStore.subscribe(
      handleUpdate
    )


  return () => {

    unsubscribe()

  }

}, [])


================================================================================
EXAMPLE 5: WEBSOCKET
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const socket =
    new WebSocket(
      socketUrl
    )


  return () => {

    socket.close()

  }

}, [
  socketUrl
])


================================================================================
EXAMPLE 6: CANCEL FETCH
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const controller =
    new AbortController()


  const loadData =
    async () => {

      try {

        const response =
          await fetch(
            '/api/data',
            {
              signal:
                controller.signal
            }
          )


        if (!response.ok) {

          throw new Error(
            `Request failed: ${response.status}`
          )
        }


        const data =
          await response.json()


        setData(
          data
        )


      } catch (error) {

        if (
          error.name !==
          'AbortError'
        ) {

          setError(
            error.message
          )

        }
      }
    }


  loadData()


  return () => {

    controller.abort()

  }

}, [])


================================================================================
WHY ABORTING IS USEFUL
================================================================================

Suppose:

userId = 10


Request starts:

GET /api/users/10


Before it completes:

userId changes to 20


New request:

GET /api/users/20


Without proper handling, responses can arrive in an unexpected order.


Example:

Request 20
 ↓
finishes first


Request 10
 ↓
finishes later


The old response could overwrite newer information if the logic does not guard
against obsolete requests.


Using cleanup with:

AbortController


helps cancel the obsolete request.


================================================================================
CLEANUP TABLE
================================================================================

+----------------------+-------------------------+---------------------------+
| RESOURCE             | SETUP                   | CLEANUP                   |
+----------------------+-------------------------+---------------------------+
| Event Listener       | addEventListener()      | removeEventListener()     |
| Timeout              | setTimeout()            | clearTimeout()            |
| Interval             | setInterval()           | clearInterval()           |
| Subscription         | subscribe()             | unsubscribe()             |
| Fetch                | fetch(signal)           | controller.abort()        |
| WebSocket            | new WebSocket()         | socket.close()            |
| MutationObserver     | observe()               | disconnect()              |
+----------------------+-------------------------+---------------------------+


================================================================================
CLEANUP DOES NOT MEAN EVERY EFFECT NEEDS CLEANUP
================================================================================

Example:

useEffect(() => {

  document.title =
    'Grocery List'

}, [])


This may not require cleanup for the application.


Cleanup is needed when the Effect establishes something that should be:

• Removed

• Disconnected

• Cancelled

• Reversed


