PART 9: COMMON useEffect PATTERNS
================================================================================

PATTERN 1: SYNCHRONIZE ON MOUNT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

useEffect(() => {

  connect()


  return () => {

    disconnect()

  }

}, [])


================================================================================
PATTERN 2: SYNCHRONIZE WHEN PROP CHANGES
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  loadUser(
    userId
  )

}, [
  userId
])


================================================================================
PATTERN 3: EVENT LISTENER
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  window.addEventListener(
    'resize',
    handleResize
  )


  return () => {

    window.removeEventListener(
      'resize',
      handleResize
    )

  }

}, [])


================================================================================
PATTERN 4: MULTIPLE DEPENDENCIES
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  loadResults(
    userId,
    filter
  )

}, [
  userId,
  filter
])


================================================================================
PATTERN 5: CONDITIONAL EFFECT LOGIC
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  if (!userId) {

    return
  }


  loadUser(
    userId
  )

}, [
  userId
])


The Hook itself is still called unconditionally.

The logic INSIDE the Effect is conditional.


GOOD:

useEffect(() => {

  if (userId) {

    ...

  }

}, [
  userId
])


AVOID:

if (userId) {

  useEffect(...)

}


================================================================================
PATTERN 6: MULTIPLE EFFECTS
================================================================================

Use separate Effects when they synchronize with different external systems.


Example:

useEffect(() => {

  // User API synchronization

}, [
  userId
])


useEffect(() => {

  // Resize listener

  return () => {

    // Listener cleanup

  }

}, [])


useEffect(() => {

  document.title =
    user
      ? `User: ${user.name}`
      : 'Users'

}, [
  user
])


================================================================================
PATTERN 7: LOCAL STORAGE
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  localStorage.setItem(
    'groceries',
    JSON.stringify(
      groceryItems
    )
  )

}, [
  groceryItems
])


Flow:

groceryItems Changes
      ↓
Effect
      ↓
localStorage Updated


================================================================================
PATTERN 8: WEBSOCKET CONNECTION
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const socket =
    new WebSocket(
      socketUrl
    )


  return () => {

    socket.close()

  }

}, [
  socketUrl
])


================================================================================
PART 10: useEffect BEST PRACTICES
================================================================================

1. KEEP RENDERING PURE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Avoid:

function Component() {

  fetch(...)

  localStorage.setItem(...)

  window.addEventListener(...)

  return (...)
}


Rendering should calculate JSX.


================================================================================
2. USE EFFECTS FOR EXTERNAL SYNCHRONIZATION
================================================================================

Good Effect examples:

• HTTP resource tied to component state/props

• Browser event listener

• WebSocket

• Timer

• Third-party widget

• Document API


================================================================================
3. DON'T USE EFFECTS FOR SIMPLE DERIVED VALUES
================================================================================

Avoid:

const [
  total,
  setTotal
] = useState(0)


useEffect(() => {

  setTotal(
    items.length
  )

}, [
  items
])


Better:

const total =
  items.length


Remember Lesson 20:

DERIVE WHAT CAN BE CALCULATED.


================================================================================
4. DON'T USE EFFECT FOR A USER EVENT WHEN AN EVENT HANDLER IS ENOUGH
================================================================================

Avoid:

const [
  shouldDelete,
  setShouldDelete
] = useState(false)


useEffect(() => {

  if (shouldDelete) {

    deleteItem()

  }

}, [
  shouldDelete
])


when Delete can simply happen from:

handleDelete()


================================================================================
5. INCLUDE REACTIVE DEPENDENCIES
================================================================================

If an Effect reads:

userId


then normally:

userId


belongs in the dependency array.


Use linting support rather than manually hiding dependency warnings.


================================================================================
6. CLEAN UP EXTERNAL RESOURCES
================================================================================

If you add:

Listener


remove:

Listener


If you start:

Timer


clear:

Timer


If you subscribe:

Subscription


unsubscribe.


If you start an obsolete cancellable request:

Abort it when appropriate.


================================================================================
7. HANDLE HTTP FAILURES
================================================================================

Use:

if (!response.ok) {

  throw new Error(...)

}


because not every HTTP error causes `fetch()` itself to reject.


================================================================================
8. HANDLE RACE CONDITIONS
================================================================================

If a changing dependency starts multiple requests:

Old Request
+
New Request


ensure an obsolete response cannot incorrectly replace the latest data.


AbortController is useful for fetch-based workflows.


================================================================================
9. KEEP EFFECTS FOCUSED
================================================================================

Avoid one huge Effect that:

• Fetches data

• Adds listeners

• Updates title

• Creates WebSocket

• Starts timer


Prefer separate Effects when each represents a different synchronization
process.


================================================================================
10. DON'T OVERUSE useEffect
================================================================================

Ask:

"Am I synchronizing with something outside React?"


If:

NO


you might not need an Effect.


Examples often NOT requiring useEffect:

• Calculating totals

• Filtering an array for rendering

• Handling a button click

• Updating state from an input event

• Formatting data for display


================================================================================
11. DON'T MAKE THE EFFECT FUNCTION async
================================================================================

Avoid:

useEffect(
  async () => {

    ...

  },
  []
)


Instead:

useEffect(() => {

  const run =
    async () => {

      ...
    }


  run()

}, [])


================================================================================
12. REMOVE DEBUG LOGGING WHEN DONE
================================================================================

Temporary:

console.log(
  'Fetching'
)


can be helpful.


But permanent excessive logging:

• Creates noise

• Makes debugging harder

• Can expose unnecessary application details


Keep production behavior deliberate.


